| **Grain growth in 8YSZ / cubic zirconia is highly temperature sensitive** | **Supported wording:** “Grain growth in yttria-stabilized zirconia is thermally activated and follows a grain-growth law with an Arrhenius temperature dependence of the rate constant, making coarsening highly sensitive to annealing temperature.” | **Tekeli & Demir, 2005**, *Ceramics International* **31**, 973–980. DOI: **10.1016/j.ceramint.2004.10.011**; **Matsui, Yoshida, & Ikuhara, 2010**, *Journal of the European Ceramic Society* **30**, 1679–1690. DOI: **10.1016/j.jeurceramsoc.2010.01.007** (pqac-00000000, pqac-00000001) | **Tekeli & Demir 2005** directly measures static grain growth in **8 mol% yttria-stabilized cubic zirconia (8YSCZ)** annealed at **1400–1600 °C** for **10–100 h**, reporting **grain-growth exponent n = 2** and **activation energy Q = 289 kJ mol⁻¹** for undoped 8YSCZ; this is the best composition-specific citation for Arrhenius grain-growth kinetics in cubic zirconia. **Matsui et al. 2010** directly reports YSZ grain-growth activation energies of **588 and 640 kJ mol⁻¹** for identified mechanisms, providing strong corroboration that YSZ grain growth is thermally activated with large activation barriers. |
| **Direct graphite–alumina contact was avoided because of a eutectic reaction lowering the interface melting temperature to ~1800 °C** | **Recommended correction:** “Direct contact between graphite and alumina was avoided because alumina is not inert to carbon at very high temperature, especially under vacuum or low oxygen potential: the interface can undergo **carbothermal reduction**, generating **CO** and Al-bearing vapor species and forming **Al₄C₃** and aluminum oxycarbides (e.g., **Al₄O₄C**), which degrade the interface. We therefore avoid describing this as a simple Al₂O₃–C eutectic at ~1800 °C.” | **Walker & Fruehan, 2013**, *Metallurgical and Materials Transactions B* **44**, 853–861. DOI: **10.1007/s11663-013-9839-3**; **Kruesi, Galvez, Halmann, & Steinfeld, 2011**, *Metallurgical and Materials Transactions B* **42**, 254–260. DOI: **10.1007/s11663-010-9461-6**; **Cox & Pidgeon, 1963**, *Canadian Journal of Chemistry* **41**, 671–683. DOI: **10.1139/v63-095**; **Lihrmann, Zambetakis, & Daire, 1989**, *Journal of the American Ceramic Society* **72**, 1704–1709. DOI: **10.1111/j.1151-2916.1989.tb06306.x**; **Fernández-González et al., 2018**, *Solar Energy* **170**, 520–540. DOI: **10.1016/j.solener.2018.05.065** (pqac-00000002) | The currently available evidence supports **reactive carbothermal degradation**, not a simple binary “Al₂O₃–graphite eutectic at ~1800 °C.” **Walker & Fruehan 2013**, **Kruesi et al. 2011**, and **Cox & Pidgeon 1963** are the key thermodynamic/experimental carbothermal-reduction papers to cite for Al₂O₃–C reactivity under vacuum/low oxygen potential. **Lihrmann et al. 1989** is the appropriate phase-equilibria paper for the reacted **Al₂O₃–Al₄C₃/oxycarbide** subsystem, showing that oxycarbides must be considered rather than invoking pristine Al₂O₃–C eutectic melting. **Fernández-González et al. 2018** is a useful review-level support for the statement that bulk carbothermal reduction of alumina typically requires about **2100–2300 °C**, while under vacuum lower-temperature pathways still involve **Al₄C₃, Al₄O₄C, and volatile Al₂O** rather than a simple eutectic-melting mechanism. |
